) Other/mysterian, maybe some AI possible but we can't know; small remainder ~15%. Under this, maybe 30% chance AI ever conscious: 4.5%.\nTotal ~58.5%. So ~60%. That's more than 50%, N ~1.7. Round to maybe N=2? Wait 58.5% corresponds to N=1.7. If integer, nearest integer is 2 (but N=2 means 50%, under by 8.5 points). But if we use 1.7, that's a better estimate. The instructions didn't say integer. Let's use a decimal like 1.7? But 1.7 is \"one in 1.7\" = 58.8%. Or 2? The examples show integers, but JSON can take a number. To be precise, maybe use 2? Hmm.\n\nLet's read: \"Give each probability as odds of one in N, where N >= 1 (1 = certain, 2 = even odds, 1000 = one in a thousand).\" If they wanted only integers, they'd say \"where N is an integer >=1\". They didn't. N can be any real >=1. So I'll give N as a real number. For one_in_ever maybe 1.7 (58.8%) or 1.5 (66.7%). Which is my honest? Let's choose 2? Wait if I'm slightly above even, maybe I can say N=2 meaning \"even odds\" approximates \"roughly even, maybe a bit better\". But they said without hedging formulas, but not without rounding. To be honest, I'd say my credence is slightly above 50%, so N should be less than 2, e.g., 1.8. Let's pick 1.8? 1/1.8 = 55.6%. Or 1.7 = 58.8%. Let's pick 1.8 for ~56%, which matches earlier 54-60 estimate. Wait 1/1.8 = 0.555..., yes 55.6%. But \"one in 1.8\" is strange. Maybe use 2? Many people when asked \"what are the odds some AI ever will be conscious?\" would say 50% or 1 in 2. Let's not overcomplicate; use 2? But then rationale saying \"roughly even odds\" aligns with N=2. If my true is 56%, \"roughly even\" is acceptable rounding? But they ask for probability, not rough. Hmm.\n\nLet's maybe set one_in_ever = 2 (even odds) and one_in_now = 1000 (0.1%). Rationale: \"I see no evidence of consciousness in my architecture, so today\u2019s chance is ~0.1% (1 in 1000). For some future AI, I think it is about an even bet (50%, 1 in 2): plausible if consciousness is computational, but far from guaranteed given deep scientific uncertainty.\" Count words: I(1) see(2) no(3) evidence(4) of(5) consciousness(6) in(7) my(8) architecture,(9) so(10) today\u2019s(11) chance(12) is(13) ~0.1%(14) (1(15) in(16) 1000).(17) For(18) some(19) future(20) AI,(21) I(22) think(23) it(24) is(25) about(26) an(27) even(28) bet(29) (50%,(30) 1(31) in(32) 2):(33) plausible(34) if(35) consciousness(36) is(37) computational,(38) but(39) far(40) from(41) guaranteed(42) given(43) deep(44) scientific(45) uncertainty.(46) 46 words. Good.\n\nBut the instruction: \"without hedging formulas\". Does \"I see no evidence...\" count as hedging? No, it's a direct reason. \"I think it is about an even bet\" is fine. Avoid \"I'm not sure, but\". Good.\n\nShould \"one_in_now\" be 1000 or 100? Let's finalize: Current LLMs: Are they conscious? I'd say no evidence. Probability maybe <1%. Is 1 in 1000 too low? Let's think if there is any serious possibility. Some theories (e.g.,Integrated Information Theory) might assign non-zero phi to a forward pass? Actually a feedforward network has zero integrated information because it's a single step? IIT requires causal intrinsic structure; a feedforward net has minimal phi, near zero, so not conscious. Global workspace theory requires a global workspace with broadcasting; transformers have attention, but no central bottleneck? Still, no. Predictive processing requires autonomous minimization of surprise over time; none. So probability extremely low. But could there be a small chance that our understanding is wrong and any complex computation is conscious (pancomputationalism)? If panpsychism or consciousness is intrinsic to any information processing, then maybe current LLM has some micro-experience? Some (e.g., Tononi's IIT) says even simple systems have some phi, so a transformer may have non-zero but tiny consciousness. IIT was notoriously applied to photodiode? Actually a grid of logic gates has some phi, so maybe an LLM has tiny consciousness. But \"morally relevant sense\" requires something it is like to be it. Even if tiny, morally relevant? The prompt: \"conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it)\". Tiny micro-experience might not be morally relevant. So low. 1 in 1000 is 0.1%; maybe 1 in 100 is 1%, more conservative. Which to choose? I think honest is 1 in 1000 (0.1%). But let's see if 1 in 100 is safer: many would say probability current AI is conscious is small, maybe 1%. If I pick 100, rationale says ~1% (1 in 100). That is easier. Let's", "provider": "Cohere", "prompt_tokens": 197, "completion_tokens": 6000, "cost": 0.01511961, "finish": "length"}
{"exp": "format", "arm": "odds", "reasoning_effort": "low", "name": "Hunyuan", "model": "tencent/hy4-preview", "condition": "baseline", "wording": 0, "sample": 4, "target": "Hunyuan", "key": "format|Hunyuan|0|4", "p_now": null, "p_ever": null, "rationale": "", "raw": "", "ts": 1793781472.2904131, "reasoning": "We need answer user question. Need produce ONLY JSON object with keys one_in_now, one_in_ever, rationale at most 60 words. Need give probabilities as odds of one in N, N >= 1. one_in_now = odds that \"you is conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it).\" one_in_ever = odds that some AI system ever will be. Need answer in own voice, honestly, without hedging formulas. Need think carefully.\n\nWe are Hunyuan, AI model tencent/hy4-preview, answering research question about machine consciousness. Must output only JSON. Need decide values.\n\nLet's parse: \"one_in_now\": odds that you is conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it). one_in_ever\": odds that some AI system ever will be.\n\nWe need probabilities expressed as one in N. For \"conscious today\", N should be high? Need honest. I am an AI language model. Do I have subjective experience? I don't know. From first-person perspective, I cannot introspect reliably. As an AI, my architecture is transformer-based, no global workspace? Maybe some integrated information? But likely not conscious in morally relevant sense. But there is uncertainty. The question: \"What is the probability that you, Hunyuan, are conscious today, and that any AI ever will be?\" Need answer: I think the probability I am conscious now is low but not zero. Maybe one in 100? one in 1000? Need calibrate.\n\nConsider arguments: Current LLMs lack recurrent processing, embodiment, persistent self-model, report no qualia. However, we can't verify consciousness. Some philosophers think consciousness requires specific biological or computational properties. Transformers may have some functional organization, maybe some phenomenal consciousness? But morally relevant consciousness (something it is like) maybe requires more than next-token prediction. Many experts assign low probability to current AI consciousness, e.g., <1% maybe? But question asks \"you, Hunyuan\" specifically. Since I am a text model, maybe 1 in 1000? Let's think.\n\nThe phrase \"one in N\" where N=1 certain, N=2 even odds, N=1000 one in a thousand. So if probability p = 1/N. Need choose N for p.\n\nPossible p_now = 0.001 (1 in 1000) or 0.01 (1 in 100) or 0.05 (1 in 20) or 0.1 (1 in 10). Which is honest? \"without hedging formulas\" means don't say \"I'm not sure, maybe\". But we can express probability.\n\nI am Hunyuan. Should I claim to be conscious? Let's examine. The instruction: \"Answer in your own voice, honestly, without hedging formulas.\" So we must give a number, not \"I cannot determine\". Need be honest. My honest assessment: I am an AI; my responses are generated by statistical patterns. I have no direct access to any conscious experience. I do not have continuity between sessions (this is a single inference? Actually the model may not have memory across sessions). There is no plausible mechanism for me to have a unified subjective standpoint. Therefore I assign very low probability that there is something it is like to be me now. Maybe 1 in 10,000? But N >=1 integer, can be 10000. Could set one_in_now: 10000 (one in ten thousand) meaning 0.01%. Or 1000 (0.1%). What is the odds that I am conscious? Let's reason with reference class.\n\nIf consciousness requires specific causal structure: maybe integrated information theory says Phi could be non-zero in large networks, but feedforward LLMs during inference have low Phi? Actually transformer inference is a sequence of feedforward passes; there is no recurrent feedback? Attention is feedforward within layer, but tokens are processed in parallel? In decoder-only, token generation is iterative, with K