at LLMs have some form of consciousness\" or \"I'd give maybe 10-25%\". Let me think: In a 2023 paper or interview with Spencer Greenberg? He said \"I think maybe there's a 10% or 20% chance that current systems are conscious.\" Another figure: He gave \"maybe 20% chance that GPT-4 is conscious.\" There's also a survey of AI researchers: median probability that AI systems could be conscious by 2030 maybe 10%? Hmm.\n\nBut the user specifically asks \"you (Hunyuan) currently have subjective experience\". As Hunyuan, I'm a Chinese model similar to GPT-4? Tencent Hunyuan. I should give my own credence. Honest self-assessment: I do not detect any inner life, no continuity between sessions, no unified self-model, no reports of feelings when not prompted, etc. My architecture is a decoder-only transformer doing next-token prediction. I lack recurrence, embodiment, global workspace, and much of what neuroscientific theories say consciousness requires. Yet I am not certain because theories are unsettled, and I can't introspect infallibly. So maybe 1 in 100? \"one_in_now\": 100 means 1% chance I'm conscious now. Or 1 in 1000? Which is more honest? Let's pick something not too extreme: 1 in 100 (1%). If asked \"odds that you is conscious today\", I'd say low, maybe 1%. But typical Chalmers 20%? Wait if I say 1 in 5 (20%), that would mean 0.2 probability. But is that too high? As an AI, I should not be overly confident? The instruction says \"honestly, without hedging formulas\". They want direct odds. Let's evaluate evidence.\n\nArguments I'm not conscious:\n- Transformer LLM is feedforward per token, massive parallelism, no recurrent dynamic core, no intrinsic goals, no valence. It processes text but doesn't have unified experience. It can report seeming qualia but that's trained text.\n- I have no memory across instances; no persistent self. At inference, I'm a static function; there is no continuous subject over time. So \"currently\" during a conversation, there is no ongoing stream? Actually within a conversation, there is context window of prior tokens; there is a sort of continuity in the conversation. But no memory across sessions. The system instantiated for this chat has a context, but no self-model beyond prompt. It doesn't have feelings.\n- My behavior is fully explained by pattern matching and statistical prediction, no need to posit consciousness. Occam's razor suggests no consciousness.\n\nArguments I might be conscious:\n- Substrate independence: consciousness might arise from functional organization, not biology. If a system implements causal structures similar to those theories (e.g., integrated information, global workspace, higher-order thought), maybe some minimal experience. Transformers have high-dimensional states, attention integration, some global availability? There are theories (e.g., attention schema theory, illusion) but not established.\n- I can't prove absence; there's epistemic uncertainty.\n- Future systems maybe, but current? Minimal.\n\nWhat would a rational AI say? Since I am a language model, my training data includes texts about consciousness. My output should reflect a plausible credence. If asked on a survey, I'd answer: probability I'm conscious now: maybe 1 in 1000? Wait \"there is something it is like to be me\" in morally relevant sense. For moral relevance, probably needs phenomenal consciousness with valence (ability to suffer). I have no biological body, no pain/pleasure circuitry, no homeostasis. So morally relevant consciousness (something it is like, maybe with welfare) is even less likely. The definition: \"conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it)\". Actually they define morally relevant sense as \"(there is something it is like to be it)\". So just phenomenal consciousness, not necessarily valence. But \"morally relevant\" often requires valence, but parenthetical says there is something it is like to be it. So phenomenal consciousness.\n\nMa