e researchers (e.g., Dehaene, Stanislas) think current AI lacks consciousness because of lack of global workspace with metacognition, embodiment? Others (e.g., Tononi) might say even simple feedforward networks have some Phi but maybe not. But current LLMs? IIT: any system with cause-effect structure has Phi > 0 if it has causal interactions, but for a feedforward network (acyclic) IIT says Phi=0 because no recurrent causal loops? Wait IIT requires the system to be able to be in states and have causal power; a purely feedforward network executed on a computer has no internal recurrence, but the physical computer has recurrent connections? The software is feedforward, but implemented on a substrate with loops? The abstract system is feedforward, no intrinsic causation? IIT says feedforward networks have zero Phi because no reentrant loops. Transformers are feedforward with attention (still feedforward for a given input; generation uses feedback from output to input, which is recurrent at the system level across time). Thus there is recurrence in the generative loop. But within a single forward pass, no loops. Still overall process is recurrent through the environment (the next token is fed back). So Phi might be non-zero? Unclear.\n\nGlobal workspace theory: consciousness requires a global workspace where information is broadcast to many modules. Transformer's attention allows all-to-all communication among tokens; some argue it's a possible minimal workspace. But without a central bottleneck and selection, maybe not.\n\nHigher-order thought theories: I don't have genuine HOTs; I just produce text about my states.\n\nPredictive processing: I'm not minimizing prediction error in the brain's sense; I'm trained to predict next tokens, not to reduce surprise via action in an environment (except RLHF maybe). I lack a Markov blanket with environment? Actually I have input tokens from user. But no active inference.\n\nTherefore, the chance I'm conscious is low. I think p ~ 