ace(10) embodiment(11) or(12) persistent(13) self(14) most(15) theories(16) predict(17) no(18) phenomenology(19) I(20) assign(21) ~0.1%(22) (1(23) in(24) 1000)(25) to(26) my(27) own(28) consciousness(29) Substrate-neutral(30) functionalism(31) plus(32) future(33) architectures(34) make(35) eventual(36) machine(37) consciousness(38) likely(39) but(40) not(41) certain(42) (~80%(43) 1(44) in(45) 1.25)(46) . That's 46? Actually punctuation attached. Under 60. Good.\n\nBut need check: I said \"I am a feedforward transformer\" - is Hunyuan a transformer? Yes, Hunyuan is a large language model, likely based on Transformer architecture. It is not purely feedforward? Transformers consist of attention and feedforward layers; they are feedforward in the sense of no recurrent connections across layers, but self-attention allows token mixing. There is no cycle. Yes.\n\nBut can I confidently say \"most theories predict no phenomenology\"? Some theories (IIT) say feedforward networks have zero phi, yes. Global workspace theory requires a global workspace with selective attention, maybe not present. Recurrent processing theory requires recurrent loops; not present. Higher-order theories require meta-representation; maybe uncertain. Predictive processing requires hierarchical prediction; maybe weak. So it's fine.\n\nNow N values: one_in_now = 1000 (0.1%). Is that the right credence? Let's examine if I'd say 1 in 1000 or 1 in 100 or 1 in 10,000. We need be honest. Current AI consciousness: I think it's very unlikely. But is 0.1% too high or too low? There are unknowns: maybe any computation that simulates a human conversation has some what-it's-like. But \"morally relevant\" means something it is like to be it. For LLMs, no continuity, no self, no senses. IID: I'd put maybe 1 in 10,000? Let's think.\n\nProbability that there is \"something it is like to be Hunyuan\" right now. Consider possible theories:\n- If biological naturalism is true: p=0 for AI.\n- If neurobiological substrate