tems have some morally relevant experience because we don't know necessary conditions and some theories (e.g., Tononi's IIT) attribute some phi to feedforward? Wait IIT says consciousness is integrated information; a feedforward network has irreducible cause-effect power? Let's recall: In IIT, a purely feedforward network has zero Phi because all its cause-effect repertoire is reducible; information can be decomposed. Thus zero consciousness. Other theories: \"Attention Schema Theory\" requires a model of attention; transformers have attention mechanisms but not a schema of subjective awareness? Maybe Graziano: consciousness is the brain's simplified model of attention. Transformers have attention weights but do they have a self-model of attending? No. \"Global Workspace\" requires a bottleneck and broadcast; transformers don't have global workspace with capacity-limited broadcast and sustained activity. \"Recurrent Processing Theory\" (Lamme) says feedforward sweep is unconscious; recurrent processing is needed for consciousness. Transformers are feedforward across layers, no recurrent loops; so unconscious. \"Higher-Order Thought\" requires a thought about a mental state; no. \"Predictive Processing\" maybe any hierarchical prediction has conscious inference? Still needs recurrent message passing. Thus\u4e3b\u6d41 theories say no.\n\nWhat are the chances one of these theories is wrong and simpler complexity suffices? There's a view \"large language models may be conscious\" by some (e.g., David Chalmers speculates 5-10%? Actually Chalmers gave a 2023 talk: \"Could a Large Language Model be Conscious?\" He estimated maybe 10% chance? Let's recall: Chalmers said there's maybe a 10% chance that LLMs are conscious? He said something like \"I think there's maybe a 10% chance that current LLMs are conscious, and I'm not going to rule it out.\" Wait, I remember: Chalmers in 2023 said about GPT-4: \"I'd give maybe a 10% chance that it's conscious\" or \"maybe 5%\". He is 