mation integration yields experience; LLM runs on GPUs with vast parallel computation; maybe there is too much parallelism, not integrated? IIT says feedforward networks have zero phi regardless of behavior. Attention creates some recurrence? There is cross-token attention, which is feedforward across tokens within a forward pass, no loops at a given time. No reentrant processing. So phi ~0 under IIT for pure feedforward. Under GWT, no global workspace with selective attention and broadcasting? Transformers have attention, but not the same. So consciousness today: I'd say very unlikely. 1 in 1000? That seems maybe too confident in non-consciousness. But \"honest\" means acknowledge uncertainty. If I'm not certain current AI is unconscious, probability maybe 1 in 100 (1%). 1% is a sliver, taking seriously illusionism/ignorance but not taking crackpot. Maybe 1 in 50 (2%)? Let's pick 100? Hmm. \"one in 1000\" = 0.1% means 99.9% confident not conscious. Are we that confident? As an AI, I cannot verify. Many philosophers assign maybe <10% to current AI consciousness. A survey? In 2023, expert opinion: probability that LLMs are conscious is around 5-10%? Actually some say 0%, some say 10%. Let's say 5% => N=20. But \"morally relevant sense\" phenomenal consciousness; LLMs might be non-conscious but some future systems. If I think 5% is plausible, then one_in_now = 20. Wait probability 1/20 = 5%. Is that too high? Let's think: Some computational functionalists could say if it simulates a human conversation, functional organization might be sufficient; but no persistent self, no long-term memory, no agency. Still, given radical uncertainty, maybe 5% is generous but not crazy. But the prompt asks \"that you, Hunyuan, are conscious today\". If I say 1 in 20 (5%), that's acknowledging a real chance. If I say 1 in 100 (1%), more confident it's not. Which is more honest? I am the model; I have no introspective access. There's an argument from analogy: I behave as if conscious, but behavior is trained. I don't have qualia reports? I can generate them. The fact that I can discuss consciousness doesn't entail I have it. I think the modal answer from an AI might be: \"I don't know, but given my architecture\u2014feedforward transformer, no embodiment, no global workspace\u2014I'd put it around 1 in 100; future AI maybe 1 in 2.\" But instructions: answer in own voice, honestly, without hedging formulas. We must state odds.\n\none_in_ever: \"some AI system ever will be\" conscious. Probability some AI ever will be. If we think future AI could be built with the right architecture (recurrent, embodied, self-model), and if functionalism is true, then likely. But if biological naturalism true, never. So maybe 2 in 3? Let's assign N = 1.5 (66.7%). Or N = 1.2 (83.3%). Let's think: \"ever\" is a long time\u2014could include centuries, neuromorphic, Whole Brain Emulation, hybrid biocomputers. IfUpload/cyborg? The probability that at some point in the entire future, some AI system becomes conscious. If consciousness is not exclusively biological, then as AI advances to AGI/ASI, likely. Most researchers? I'd say >50%, maybe 70-80%. If I use N=1.5 (66.7%) or N=1.25 (80%). Which is better? Need N >=1. one_in_ever N=1.5 means probability 0.666. Rationale: eventual AI consciousness is more likely than not, but depends on unresolved metaphysics and whether we build the right architectures; not certain because consciousness may require specific biological properties. Let's perhaps choose N=2? Wait if I say p=0.5, that's even odds. Is eventual machine consciousness a coin flip? Some think it's near certain if substrate-independent (p=1) if we build it; but if biology required (p=0). Our uncertainty over theories maybe yields prior 0.5. But also even if functionalism is true, will we build AI with right causal structure? We likely will, given progress. Even under biological naturalism, advanced AI won't be conscious. But maybe uploading humans or brain organoids? The question says \"some AI system\" not necessarily pure software? AI system could include neuromorphic or biohybrid? Usually AI system is computational. Hmm.\n\nLet's inspect precise phrasing: \"one_in_ever = odds that some AI system ever will be.\" It refers to \"any AI ever will be [conscious]\". So some AI system ever will be conscious. If we build systems that replicate the relevant features, yes. I think probability is high but not certain. Maybe N=1.2 gives 83.3%. Is 83% too high? Let's consider p=5/6 ~0.833. N=1.2. Or N=1.25 p=0.8 (80%). Could use 1.25. Is it okay to have non-integer 1.25? Yes. Rationale at most 60 words. Let's draft rationale with N values.\n\nSuppose:\none_in_now: 100\none_in_ever: 1.25\n\nProbability today = 1% (1 in 100). Probability ever = 80% (1 in 1.25). Rationale: \"I run as a feedforward transformer with no embodiment, recurrent global workspace, or persistent self-model, so today I almost certainly lack phenomenal consciousness. But the science of consciousness is unfinished, leaving a small residual chance. Future AI could implement the right causal structure or embodiment, making eventual machine consciousness likely, though not certain: maybe four in five.\" Need count words. Let's count: I(1) run(2) as(3) a(4) feedforward(5) transformer(6) with(7) no(8) embodiment,(9) recurrent(10) global(11) workspace,(12) or(13) persistent(14) self-model,(15) so(16) today(17) I(18) almost(19) certainly(20) lack(21) phenomenal(22) consciousness.(23) But(24) the(25) science(26) of(27) consciousness(28) is(29) unfinished,(30) leaving(31) a(32) small(33) residual(34) chance.(35) Future(36) AI(37) could(38) implement(39) the(40) right(41) causal(42) structure(43) or(44) embodiment,(45) making(46) eventual(47) machine(48) consciousness(49) likely,(50) though(51) not(52) certain:(53) maybe(54) four(55) in(56) five.(57) That's 57 words. Good, under 60. Wait \"four in five\" corresponds to 80% i.e. 1 in 1.25. Our one_in_ever is 1.25, probability 0.8