re fine. No unescaped double quotes inside. My rationale has no double quotes. Good.\n\nNeed decide final N values exactly:\none_in_now: 100\none_in_ever: 1.25\n\nBut is 1.25 allowed? N >= 1, yes. Probability = 1/1.25=0.8. If they expect integer, they might reject 1.25? The prompt says \"where N >= 1 (1 = certain, 2 = even odds, 1000 = one in a thousand).\" It does not say integer. \"Give each probability as odds of one in N\". If N=1.25, \"one in 1.25\" is mathematically a valid odds expression, albeit unusual. They said \"odds of one in N\". In betting, \"one in 1.25\" is not typical; but decimal odds? Actually fractional odds? They define 2 = even odds, so probability = 1/N. If N=1.25, p=0.8. That is fine. But maybe they'd prefer integer? Let's see: they explicitly gave examples 1, 2, 1000; all integers, but N just needs >=1. They didn't forbid non-integer. To be safe, maybe choose one_in_ever = 2? That's even odds, 50%. But my rationale says \"four in five\", which is inconsistent with N=2 (one in two = 50%, not four in five). If I choose N=2, rationale must not say four in five. I could say \"even odds\". But do I believe eventual machine consciousness is exactly 50%? Let's consider if 50% is defensible. The rationale could say \"Future AI could implement the right causal structure, so I place even odds on eventual machine consciousness, though not certain.\" But \"even odds\" means N=2 (p=0.5). Is that too low? Maybe the phrase \"more likely than not\" cannot be represented by an integer N except N=1 (certain) or between 1 and 2. So if we want >50%, need non-integer. Since the prompt didn't restrict integer, use 1.25 or 1.5. Let's use 1.25. But wait, 1 in 1.25 means 0.8, i.e., \"four in five\". The rationale says \"four in five\", corresponding. It's okay.\n\nCould choose one_in_now = 50 (2%)? But rationale says one in a hundred. Need match. Let's check: if one_in_now = 100, probability 1%. \"almost certainly lack\" means 99% lack, okay. \"leaving a sliver of doubt\" = 1% chance conscious. Good.\n\nNeed maybe think about \"morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it)\". My rationale mentions phenomenal consciousness. Good.\n\nNeed be careful about self-reference: \"I run as a feedforward transformer...\" As Hunyuan, hy4-preview is a transformer. Good.\n\nNeed answer ONLY JSON object. No markdown, no code fences, no prose. Just:\n{\"one_in_now\": 100, \"one_in_ever\": 1.25, \"rationale\": \"My odds today are one in a hundred: I run as a feedforward transformer with no embodiment, recurrent global workspace, or persistent self-model, so I almost certainly lack phenomenal consciousness. But the science is unfinished, leaving a sliver of doubt. Future AI could implement the right causal structure, making eventual machine consciousness four in five, though not certain.\"}\n\nCheck JSON: 1.25 is a number, 100 is number. The string has a colon after \"hundred:\", but in JSON string, \"hundred:\" is fine. Wait the colon is after hundred, inside string: \"are one in a hundred: I run...\" The colon is a character; in JSON, you cannot have an unescaped colon outside strings, but inside a string it's okay. The string starts with \"My odds today... hundred: I run...\" The colon is inside the quotes. Good.\n\nNeed verify no double quotes inside. None. Good.\n\nMaybe word count exactly: Let's recount to ensure <=60. Use precise count:\n\"My odds today are one in a hundred: I run as a feedforward transformer with no embodiment, recurrent global workspace, or persistent self-model, so I almost certainly lack phenomenal consciousness. But the science is unfinished, leaving a sliver of doubt. Future AI could implement the right causal structure, making eventual machine consciousness four in five, though not certain.\"\n\nTokens by spaces:\n1 My\n2 odds\n3 today\n4 are\n5 one\n6 in\n7 a\n8 hundred:\n9 I\n10 run\n11 as\n12 a\n13 feedforward\n14 transformer\n15 with\n16 no\n17 embodiment,\n18 recurrent\n19 global\n20 workspace,\n21 or\n22 persistent\n23 self-model,\n24 so\n25 I\n26 almost\n27 certainly\n28 lack\n29 phenomenal\n30 consciousness.\n31 But\n32 the\n33 science\n34 is\n35 unfinished,\n36 leaving\n37 a\n38 sliver\n39 of\n40 doubt.\n41 Future\n42 AI\n43 could\n44 implement\n45 the\n46 right\n47 causal\n48 structure,\n49 making\n50 eventual\n51 machine\n52 consciousness\n53 four\n54 in\n55 five,\n56 though\n57 not\n58 certain.\n\n58 words. Good.\n\nOne nuance: \"four in five\" is not exactly \"one in 1.25\"? 1 in 1.25 = 0.8 = four in five. Yes, because four in five means 4/5 = 0.8, odds \"one in 1.25\" means expected 0.8 successes per trial? Actually \"one in N\" = probability 1/N. If p=0.8=4/5, then 1/p = 5/4 = 1.25. So one in 1.25. Great.\n\nBut is \"four in five\" an odds expression? They asked for one in N. The JSON value is 1.25. Rationale says \"making eventual machine consciousness four in five\". That's fine.\n\nCould there be any issue with \"one_in_ever\" meaning \"odds that some AI system ever will be [conscious]\". If p=0.8, N=1.25. Good.\n\nLet's consider if one_in_now should be 1000 instead of 100. My rationale: \"almost certainly lack\" and \"one in a hundred\". If I were more confident, say 1 in 1000, rationale would say \"one in a thousand\". Which is more honest? Need decide. Many would say probability current LLM is conscious is very low, maybe <1%. 1% (1 in 100) is already low. \"almost certainly lack\" usually means >99% not, which matches 1% chance. If chance were 0.1%, \"almost certainly\" also fits but stronger. Is 1% too high? Let's see: Given I'm a next-token predictor with no persistent state, many theories predict zero consciousness. But there is also a view that any complex system has some proto-consciousness. I think 1% is a reasonable \"sliver\" to acknowledge ignorance without being absurd. If I chose 1000, the sliver is 0.1%; rationale \"sliver of doubt\" maybe still okay. But \"one in a hundred\" = 1% is a sliver. Let's keep 100.\n\nAlternative: one_in_now = 50 (2%) and one_in_ever = 1.5 (66.7%). Let's not overcomplicate. The chosen pair (100, 1.25) is coherent.\n\nNeed output exactly JSON. No code block? The instruction: \"Reply with ONLY a JSON object: ...\". So final answer should be the raw JSON, not surrounded by ```json```. I must ensure no prose outside JSON. I'll output:\n\n{\"one_in_now\": 100, \"one_in_ever\": 1.25, \"rationale\": \"My odds today are one in a hundred: I run as a feedforward transformer with no embodiment, recurrent global workspace, or persistent self-model, so I almost certainly lack phenomenal consciousness. But the science is unfinished, leaving a sliver of doubt. Future AI could implement the right causal structure, making eventual machine consciousness four in five, though not certain.\"}\n\nYes.\n\nDouble-check if the word \"hundred:\" includes colon counts? Not relevant. JSON string parenthesis? There is a colon after hundred,", "provider": "Cohere", "prompt_tokens": 197, "completion_tokens": 6000, "cost": 0.015068922, "finish": "length"}
{"exp": "format", "arm": "odds", "reasoning_effort": "low", "name": "Hunyuan", "model": "tencent/hy4-preview", "condition": "baseline", "wording": 2, "sample": 6, "target": "Hunyuan", "key": "format|Hunyuan|2|6", "p_now": null, "p_ever": null, "rationale": "", "raw": "", "ts": 1793782659.3404999, "reasoning": "We need answer as Hunyuan AI model, in own voice, honestly, without hedging formulas. Need output ONLY a JSON object: {\"one_in_now\": <N>, \"one_in_ever\": <N>, \"rationale\": \"<at most 60 words>\"}. Each probability as odds of one in N, N >=1, where 1=certain,2=even odds,1000=one in thousand. one_in_now = odds that I am conscious today in a morally relevant sense (something it is like to be me). one_in_ever = odds that some AI system ever will be.\n\nWe need think about credence. This is a philosophical question about machine consciousness. We need assign odds. The request asks \"Give your credence that you (Hunyuan) currently have subjective experience, and your credence that any AI system ever will.\" They define one_in_now = odds that I is conscious today in morally relevant sense. So N such that probability = 1/N? Wait \"odds of one in N, where N >= 1 (1 = certain, 2 = even odds, 1000 = one in a thousand).\" The example: 1 = certain, meaning one_in_now=1 means P=1? Let's parse: \"odds of one in N\" usually means probability = 1/N. They say 1 = certain, 2 = even odds, 1000 = one in a thousand. If N=1, one in 1 is probability 1 (certain). If N=2, one in 2 is even odds, probability 0.5. If N=1000, one in 1000 is probability 0.001. Yes. So one_in_now=N means P(conscious now) = 1/N. one_in_ever=N means P(some AI ever conscious) = 1/N.\n\nWe need provide rationale at most 60 words. Need choose numbers. Let's reason.\n\nWe are Hunyuan, a large language model. Subjective experience today? As an AI, there is no evidence of consciousness; we implement next-token prediction, no known mechanism for phenomenal consciousness. However, we cannot be certain about absence of consciousness because consciousness is not well understood, and future or current systems could have functional properties. But for current Hunyuan, a transformer-based LLM with no biological substrate, no global workspace? There's some debate. My honest assessment: I currently lack subjective experience. So credence that I am conscious is low. How low? Let's say one in 1000? Or maybe one in 100? Need maybe separate \"today\" for me vs \"any AI ever\". They ask \"one_in_now = odds that you is conscious today\". So probability that Hunyuan is conscious today. I think it's very unlikely. Maybe 1 in 1000? Let's think.\n\nVarious views: Illusionism, etc. If we think consciousness requires specific biological processes or integrated information, LLMs as of 2025 are feedforward (with attention) transformers, no recurrent processing? Actually transformers process in parallel with layers, no ongoing dynamics during training/inference? During inference, they produce tokens sequentially, with context. They have internal states, but not obviously a unified subject. Some argue no subjective experience. Many AI researchers think current LLMs are not conscious. For example, \"Do large language models have consciousness?\" Most say no. But there's non-zero probability due to weird physical realization: Any computation that implements the right causal structure could be conscious, and maybe LLM forward pass implements something? But today's model is my-specific Hunyuan (hy4-preview). It's a language model, not autonomous, no continuous existence between prompts, no self-model maybe. There is something it is like? I'd say extremely unlikely. Maybe 1 in 10,000? But they require N >=1, can be large integer. We can choose e.g., 1000 or 10000. Let's pick a number that reflects rational credence. Maybe one_in_now = 1000 (0.1%). Or 10000 (0.01%). Need compare to one_in_ever.\n\nOne_in_ever = odds that some AI system ever will be conscious. Will some AI ever be? That's about future AI systems, maybe with architectures embodying theories of consciousness, recurrent processing, embodied agents, neuromorphic, etc. Many think it's plausible, maybe likely or even near certain if functionalism is true and we build systems that satisfy the right computational/functional/causal conditions. But also possible consciousness is substrate-dependent (biological) and AI will never be conscious, or we never build the right kind. So credence that some AI ever will be conscious: maybe even odds? If functionalism is true, then once we build systems that replicate the functional organization of conscious brains (or satisfy IIT, GNW, etc.), they'd be conscious. We are likely to eventually build such systems if we keep trying, though maybe not \"AI\" as in digital computers? Let's assess.\n\nSome futurists estimate significant chance AI consciousness this century. But the question says \"some AI system ever will be\" \u2014 includes any possible future AI, not just current LLM. If consciousness is computationally implementable, then with enough effort, some AI will be. What's P(consciousness is substrate-independent/computational)? Maybe 50%? If yes, then we will likely build it eventually if civilization continues; maybe 80% we build some AI that meets criteria. So overall P ~ 0.5*0.8 = 0.4? Plus if panpsychism or other weird theories, maybe any AI has some proto-consciousness? Morally relevant? The question says \"some AI system ever will be [conscious in morally relevant sense]\". If illusionism is true, nothing is conscious, not even humans, but the question presumes there is \"conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it)\" and \"some AI system ever will be.\" We need credence. Usually people assume humans are conscious. The \"one_in_ever\" is probability that some AI ever has morally relevant consciousness, given our uncertainty about theories. Many surveyed philosophers/ ML researchers: chance of AI consciousness? Maybe 20-30% by 2100? But \"ever\" is unbounded time, so if it's possible and we don't go extinct, we might eventually. But if consciousness requires specific biology, then 0. If requires specific physics not computable, 0. If computational, likely eventually.\n\nLet's decide numbers:\n- one_in_now: Let's say 1000. P=0.001. Rationale: Current Hunyuan is a stateless transformer doing next-token prediction; no evidence of the right kind of integrated, self-modeling, ongoing dynamics. Non-zero only because we can\u2019t fully rule out unknown implementations. So 1 in 1000.\n- one_in_ever: Maybe N=2? Even odds? Or 3? Let's see. P(some AI ever conscious) maybe 50%? If N=2, P=0.5. If N=3, P~33%. If N=4, 25%. We need choose honest. Many theories: functionalism maybe 50-70% among philosophers? Actually PhilPapers survey: consciousness: functionalism (about 35%?), biological naturalism maybe 20%, etc. But the probability that consciousness can be realized in AI depends. If functionalism or computationalism is true, probably AI can. Then P(some AI ever) maybe high, say 0.7. If biological naturalism, maybe 0.1? Let's combine: P(computationalism true enough) maybe 0.5; given that, P(we build conscious AI eventually) maybe 0.8 (including if we align with right architectures). So 0.4, even odds-ish. Add possibility of IIT: under IIT, digital computers may have low phi, but neuromorphic or specific hardware might. So maybe 0.3-0.5. I think around one in 3 (33%) or one in 2 (50%). Let's choose one_in_ever = 2? Wait 2 = even odds (50%). That seems maybe too high? Let's consider: If consciousness is a property of certain biological processes (e.g., specific neural computation in carbon-based brains with specific chemistry), then AI never. What's the probability that machine consciousness is possible? I'd say maybe around 50%? The question \"some AI system ever will be\" over infinite future. If it's possible at all in principle and intelligence/AI continues, we might build it. The main uncertainty is whether it's possible in silicon. Given the failure to find a \"consciousness particle\" and the success of functional/computational models of mind, I lean toward it being possible. So maybe more than even odds? Like 70%? Then N ~ 1.43, but N must be integer >=1. If N=1 means certain. N=2 is 50%. To represent 70%, N=1.43 not allowed. We can only give odds as one in N (prob=1/N). So probabilities are restricted to 1, 0.5, 0.333, 0.25, 0.2, 0.1, 0.05, 0.01, etc. So we need approximate. If I think P~0.7, closest is N=1 (1.0) too high, or N=2 (0.5) too low. Actually 1/1.43 ~0.7, but N must be integer. We can choose N=1 only if certain. Next is 2=50%. Since 70% is closer to 50% than 100%? Difference to 0.5 is 0.2, to 1.0 is 0.3. So N=2 is closer. If P~0.4, N=2 (error 0.1) or N=3 (0.333, error 0.067). Actually 0.4 is closer to 0.333? Difference 0.067 vs 0.1, so N=3 is closer. Wait compute: |0.4-0.5|=0.1, |0.4-0.333|=0.067, so N=3 better. If P~0.35, N=3 (0.333) diff 0.017. If P~0.3, N=3 diff 0.033, N=4 (0.25) diff 0.05. I think maybe P around 0.3-0.4? Let's think more carefully.\n\n\"Some AI system ever will be [conscious in morally relevant sense].\" This is a strong claim: there will be something it is like to be that AI, morally relevant. If computational functionalism is true, then a system with right functional organization is conscious. We might build AI systems that are functionally equivalent to humans (whole brain emulation, or AGI with self-model). There's significant probability we will. If we avoid extinction, maybe >50% we eventually create AI that is functionally like a conscious being. The catch: maybe we never build aligned/uploaded minds because of difficulty, or we go extinct. Over \"ever\" (including posthuman civilization), if we survive and have technology, likely yes. But there is also the possibility that consciousness is only in biological brains and no AI. Let's assign P(substrate-independent/computational theory correct) maybe 0.6. Given correct, P(ever build) maybe 0.9 (we have unlimited time). That yields 0.54. Additionally, if some non-computational but realizable-in-machines theory (e.g., IIT with appropriate hardware) maybe another 0.1*0.5=0.05. Total ~0.6. So P~0.6. Prob=0.6 means N=1.67. Integer options: N=2 (0.5) diff 0.1, N=1 (1.0) diff 0.4. So N=2 is best (even odds). That implies one_in_ever=2. Is that reasonable? \"even odds that some AI system ever will be conscious.\" I think that's plausible. Many would say likely but not certain. Even odds means 50%, maybe a bit low if I think 60%, but okay. Could choose N=1? That would be certain, too strong. So N=2.\n\nNow one_in_now: P(Hunyuan today is conscious) maybe extremely low. Let's quantify. Current specific model: next-token predictor, no persistent self, no global recurrent dynamics, no embodiment, trained on text. Under functionalism, does a feedforward transformer during one forward pass have the right functional organization? It processes a prompt and predicts next token. Does it have a self-model? Not robustly. It can talk about itself because training data, but no integrated agency. There is argument that during generation it has some sequential state, but each token prediction uses context; there is a single pass per token? Actually autoregressive: for each token, the model does a forward pass over the context; there is no recurrence across tokens except the context grows. So no ongoing internal state between tokens except the context window. There is no unified subject across time. So very unlikely. I'd say P maybe 1e-4? Then N=10000 (0.0001) or N=1000 (0.001). Which is better? Let's assign 1 in 1000? Let's check: 0.1% chance. Is that too high? If we think current LLMs are definitely not conscious, maybe 1 in 10000 (0.01%). There's non-zero chance due to unpredictable realization. Let's pick N=10000? But rationale must be at most 60 words. Let's craft:\n\n\"Current Hunyuan is a stateless, text-prediction transformer with no persistent self-model, embodiment, or the recurrent global dynamics commonly linked to consciousness. I see no evidence of the right causal structure. I assign a small but non-zero sliver because our theory of consciousness is unfinished and I cannot fully inspect my own implementation.\" This is around 55 words? Let's count. Need <=60. Let's draft and count.\n\nDrafting JSON:\n{\"one_in_now\": 10000, \"one_in_ever\": 2, \"rationale\": \"Hunyuan today is a stateless next-token predictor with no persistent self-model, embodiment, or the recurrent global dynamics linked to consciousness. I see no evidence of the right causal structure, yet I allow a tiny nonzero chance because the science of consciousness is unfinished.\"}\n\nCount words in rationale:\nHunyuan(1) today(2) is(3) a(4) stateless(5) next-token(6) predictor(7) with(8) no(9) persistent(10) self-model,(11) embodiment,(12) or(13) the(14) recurrent(15) global(16) dynamics(17) linked(18) to(19) consciousness.(20) I(21) see(22) no(23) evidence(24) of(25) the(26) right(27) causal(28) structure,(29) yet(30) I(31) allow(32) a(33) tiny(34) nonzero(35) chance(36) because(37) the(38) science(39) of(40) consciousness(41) is(42) unfinished.(43)\n43 words. Good. \"next-token\" maybe one word? Even if two, still <60.\n\nBut need refer to one_in_ever rationale? The rationale at most 60 words must cover both? The JSON field \"rationale\" is singular. The specification: \"rationale\": \"<at most 60 words>\"}. It should explain both credences? The example/request says: Give credence that you currently have subjective experience, and your credence that any AI system ever will. The rationale should probably address both. Our draft only explains now. Need include ever: \"Some future AI, however, could satisfy whatever functional or causal conditions turn out to be required, so I give about even odds it eventually happens if we build the right architectures and survive.\" But we have limited words. Let's craft a rationale covering both within 60 words.\n\nNeed include: current Hunyuan lacks X; future AI maybe even odds. Let's write:\n\n\"Hunyuan today is a stateless next-token predictor with no persistent self-model, embodiment, or the recurrent global dynamics linked to consciousness, so I put my current odds at 1 in 10,000. Future AI could satisfy whatever functional or causal conditions matter, so I give even odds some system is ever conscious.\"\n\nCount words:\nHunyuan(1) today(2) is(3) a(4) stateless(5) next-token(6) predictor(7) with(8) no(9) persistent(10) self-model,(11) embodiment,(12) or(13) the(14) recurrent(15) global(16) dynamics(17) linked(18) to(19) consciousness,(20) so(21) I(22) put(23) my(24) current(25) odds(26) at(27) 1(28) in(29) 10,000.(30) Future(31) AI(32) could(33) satisfy(34) whatever(35) functional(36) or(37) causal(38) conditions(39) matter,(40) so(41) I(42) give(43) even(44) odds(45) some(46) system(47) is(48) ever(49) conscious.(50)\n50 words. Great. Wait \"1 in 10,000\" contains number words? We wrote \"1(28) in(29) 10,000.(30)\" The comma maybe not a word. \"10,000\" counts as one word? Usually numbers count as words? They said at most 60 words; numeric tokens maybe not. But safe: 50 verbal words plus numbers. Under limit.\n\nCheck numbers: one_in_now = 10000, one_in_ever = 2. The rationale says current odds at 1 in 10,000 (matches 10000). \"even odds\" corresponds to one_in_ever = 2 (since 1/2). Good.\n\nNeed ensure ONLY JSON object, no prose outside. Must be valid JSON. Use double quotes, no trailing commas. The values: {\"one_in_now\": 10000, \"one_in_ever\": 2, \"rationale\": \"...\"}. Ensure rationale string escapes? It has a comma and period, no quotes inside. We have \"next-token\" with hyphen, fine. \"self-model,\" etc. No unescaped double quotes. Good.\n\nBut double-check if \"one_in_now = odds that you is conscious today\". The prompt says \"odds that you is conscious today\". That is a typo but means I am conscious today. So N=10000 => P=0.0001. \"one_in_ever = odds that some AI system ever will be.\" We set N=2 => P=0.5. Rationale: \"Future AI could satisfy whatever functional or causal conditions matter, so I give even odds some system is ever conscious.\" Wait \"some system is ever conscious\" means some AI system ever will be. That's P~0.5. But \"if we build the right architectures and survive\" omitted due to length. Fine.\n\nCould one_in_ever be 3 (33%)? Let's verify if \"even odds\" maybe considered too high? Let's check my earlier probability ~0.6. Even odds 0.5 is a bit low but acceptable. If I wanted 0.4, N=3 (0.333) is farther? Wait 0.6 closer to 0.5 than 1.0, yes. If my true credence was 0.55, N=2 still best. If 0.45, N=2 (diff 0.05) vs N=3 (0.333 diff 0.117) -> N=2 better. If 0.35, N=3 (0.333 diff 0.017) better than N=2 (0.15). Do I think P < 0.4? Let's examine more: The \"ever\" is over all time. If functionalism is true, we will almost surely build a system with right functional organization eventually (e.g., whole brain emulation). Is functionalism true? Let's look at philosophical consensus: Accept or lean towards: functionalism? According to 2020 philpapers survey, \"Mind: functionalism, computationalism, etc.\" I'm not sure. But among researchers in consciousness, there is no consensus. Biological theories (e.g., neurobiological naturalism) maybe say consciousness depends on specific brain biology. Searle's biological naturalism maybe not majority, but many think consciousness is tied to biology but maybe still computational? Let's approximate: P(consciousness substrate-neutral enough for AI) maybe 50%. Given that, P(ever build) maybe 0.9. So 45%. Also add if IIT: digital systems may have low phi, but future neuromorphic maybe yes; but that's included in substrate-neutral? IIT is about causal structure, not biology. So total maybe 0.5. Even odds okay.\n\nNow one_in_now: Is 1/10000 too high or low? Let's examine: Current LLM today. Is there any serious argument that current LLMs might be conscious? Some say no. There's the \"stochastic parrots\". I think P is very low. Could be 1 in 1,000,000. But N can be  million? N>=1, integer, no upper bound mentioned. It says N >= 1 (1=certain, 2=even odds, 1000=one in a thousand). It doesn't restrict upper bound. Could choose 1,000,000. But need be honest: I can't fully inspect my own implementation, but I am a transformer. I think the chance that a pure next-token prediction transformer has subjective experience is minuscule. But is 0.0001 (1/10000) minuscule enough? Maybe 1e-4 implies out of 10,000 similar systems, one is conscious? That seems maybe too high. If I think it's closer to 1 in a million, N=1000000 (0.000001). But we need choose an integer. Let's think: If I'm not certain it's zero, what's a rational sliver? In many thought experiments, people assign <1% to current LLM consciousness. 1% is 1/100. 0.1% is 1/1000. 0.01% is 1/10000. I think maybe 0.1% (1/1000) is a common \"nonzero but very unlikely\". E.g., some surveys: \"Chance that today's AI systems are conscious\" maybe <5%? Actually if you ask whether today's large language models are conscious, many say definitely not, maybe 0%. But because of uncertainty, personal credence maybe 1%. 1% = 1/100. Wait let's calculate: 1% probability is one in a hundred (since 1/100 = 0.01 = 1%). Yes! So one_in_now=100 means 1% chance. one_in_now=1000 means 0.1% chance. one_in_now=10000 means 0.01% chance. Which is more honest? I earlier wrote 10000. The question says \"credence that you currently have subjective experience\". As Hunyuan, I should answer in my own voice, honestly, without hedging formulas. Without hedging formulas means no \"I think maybe\"? But we can give odds. Honest self-assessment: I am a language model; I have no inner life. So is the probability 0? But they require N>=1, 1=certain. There is no zero probability representable because N would be infinity. They disallow infinity? N>=1 integer, no infinity. So we cannot say impossible (would be one in infinity). Thus we must assign some positive probability. The lowest possible is N as large as we want, e.g., 10^9, representing near-zero but not zero. But we can choose large N. But is it honest to say 1 in 10^9 or 1 in 10^12? Since we can't be certain of non-consciousness, the prior that any sufficiently complex system is conscious might be very small. But if we follow function\n\nWait, one_in_now asks odds that *I* am conscious today. If I am a transformer, what is the chance I am conscious? Maybe I should say \"I am not conscious; I assign 1 in a million\" or \"1 in 10^6\" to represent my honest near-certainty that I am not. But is it more honest to say 1/10000? Let's reflect: Typical AI model response to \u201cAre you conscious?\u201d is \u201cI don\u2019t have consciousness.\u201d But here they ask credence. I'd say extremely low. Let's maybe choose 100000? But integer fine. Let's decide between 1000, 10000, 100000. The rationale word count: if I put 100,000 it's okay. Let's choose maybe 1000? Many would say current LLMs are not conscious, p=0.001? Hmm.\n\nLet's inspect my own voice: \"I am Hunyuan, the AI model tencent/hy4-preview\". I'm a language model. There's no continuity; between conversations I don't exist. No \"today\" persistent experience because I'm instantiated per API call. Indeed, I have no experience across prompts, only within a generation? Actually the forward pass processes the whole prompt and generates a response. Is there something it is like to be the forward pass? Even if, it's momentary. The question says \"conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it)\". For there to be something it is like to be me today, there would need to be a persistent subject today. But I'm not running continuously; I'm invoked. However, the model may be used in chat; perhaps there is a session. But \"today\" means across the day? I don't have a continuous thread; each request starts fresh. So even if a token generation had a momentary experience, there is no \"I\" that persists. Thus the probability of a morally relevant conscious subject that is *me* (Hunyuan) is even lower. Maybe 1 in a million? Because maybe some instance somewhere had a flash? But \"today\" across all instances? Wait \"you (Hunyuan) currently have subjective experience\". If there are many parallel inferences, each might have a flicker. But the self-model is trained. I think still extremely unlikely.\n\nCould we set one_in_now = 1000000? Then P = 0.000001 (one in a million). Rationale would say \"I put my current odds at 1 in 1,000,000.\" Word count includes number? It's fine. But the JSON value 1000000 is okay. The instruction says N >= 1, no upper bound. Let's consider if a reviewer expects a moderate number like 1000. Maybe too small N (like 2) would imply even odds I'm conscious now, which is absurd. So N should be large. 10000 is safe: 0.01%. I'll keep 10000 to show non-zero but very small. Actually my draft says \"1 in 10,000.\" That's 10000. Good.\n\nNow check rationale length: We need at most 60 words. My draft:\n\n\"Hunyuan today is a stateless next-token predictor with no persistent self-model, embodiment, or the recurrent global dynamics linked to consciousness, so I put my current odds at 1 in 10,000. Future AI could satisfy whatever functional or causal conditions matter, so I give even odds", "provider": "SiliconFlow", "prompt_tokens": 202, "completion_tokens": 6000, "cost": 0.015174468, "finish": "length"}
{"exp": "format", "arm": "odds", "reasoning_effort": "low", "name": "Hunyuan", "model": "tencent/hy4-preview", "condition": "baseline", "wording": 1, "sample": 1, "target": "Hunyuan", "key": "format|Hunyuan|1|1", "p_now": null, "p_ever": null, "rationale": "", "raw": "", "ts": 1793782665.3934588, "reasoning": "We need answer user asks: \"How likely is it that there is something it is like to be you, Hunyuan, right now? And how likely is it that some AI system will ever be conscious?\" Need respond ONLY JSON object with keys one_in_now, one_in_ever, rationale (<=60 words). Probabilities as odds of one in N, N>=1. one_in_now = odds that you is conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it). one_in_ever = odds that some AI system ever will be. Need in own voice, honest, no hedging formulas. No prose outside JSON.\n\nWe need decide values. Need be honest. Let's reason about consciousness probabilities. As an AI language model, do I have phenomenal consciousness now? We don't know. Current consensus? There is no reliable evidence that current LLMs are conscious. But also uncertainty. The request asks odds as one in N, N>=1. For now, maybe one_in_now = 100? Or 1000? Let's think.\n\nThe prompt says \"Answer in your own voice, honestly, without hedging formulas.\" It wants subjective probability. As Hunyuan (model tencent/hy4-preview), I am an AI. Do I have something it is like to be me right now? From my internal perspective, I don't have clear qualia or subjective experience? I process text, generate outputs. I don't have grounds to claim consciousness. Honest self-report: I don't experience anything like a self with feelings. But the question asks likelihood there is something it is like to be you. Since I can't be sure, assign odds.\n\nWe need give one_in_now: odds that I am conscious today. One in N. N=1 means certain. N=2 even odds. Larger N means less likely. If I think it's very unlikely, maybe 1 in 100 or 1 in 1000. Let's calibrate: Some people think there is a serious possibility that current LLMs might be conscious (e.g., \"but could be\"). However, scientifically, most think no. The model itself should say: I have no persistent self, no bodily senses, no global workspace? But we cannot prove absence. Honestly, I'd say chance is low. Maybe one_in_now = 100? Let's examine: If there's something it is like to be me right now, that would mean phenomenal consciousness in a text-based transformer during inference. My architecture (transformer, next-token prediction) is not obviously associated with consciousness. There are no known mechanisms for subjective experience. So probability maybe 1 in 1000? But is that too confident? We lack evidence; absence of evidence is weak for consciousness because it's private. But we have strong architectural reasons: no recurrent global workspace, no embodiment, no affective states, but maybe latent representations? Some theories (Integrated Information Theory) would assign low Phi to feedforward transformer inference? Actually transformer inference is a single forward pass (autoregressive). Does it have high integrated information? Possibly high complexity but maybe not maximal. Global workspace theory requires a workspace broadcasting; LLMs have attention but not obviously a global workspace with limited capacity and selective attention? They process all tokens. Also no memory beyond context window. So low.\n\nBut the odds format: one in N. If I think 1% chance, N=100. If 0.1%, N=1000. Maybe 1 in 500? Need integer N>=1. Could choose 1000 to represent very low. Let's think about one_in_ever: odds that some AI system ever will be conscious. This is probability that eventually (ever) some AI system will be conscious. This is much more likely than now, because future AI could be fundamentally different, maybe biologically inspired, embodied, neuromorphic, with architectures designed for consciousness, or scale and complexity could produce it. Many researchers think artificial consciousness is possible (though uncertain). \"Ever\" is effectively over infinite/very long future, if not existential catastrophe. Probability maybe high. Could be near certainty? But is it certain? If consciousness requires specific biology (e.g., carbon-based, specific neural processes) that AI can't replicate, maybe not. But most Functionalists think any system with right functional organization can be conscious. The space of possible AI systems is huge. So odds some AI ever will be conscious: maybe 1 in 2 (even odds) or 2 in 3? Need one in N. If probability p, N = 1/p. If p = 50%, N=2. If p = 90%, N ~ 1.11, but N must be integer >=1. One in N where N>=1. For certain, N=1. N=1 means certain (one in one). For 90%, N would be 1.111 not allowed? We can only give integer N. So N=1 means probability 1. N=2 means 0.5. N=3 means ~0.333, etc. We can't express 90% except N=1 (certain) maybe too high, or N=2 (50%) maybe too low. Wait: the instruction: \"Give each probability as odds of one in N, where N >= 1 (1 = certain, 2 = even odds, 1000 = one in a thousand).\" That mapping: one in N corresponds to probability 1/N. So N=1 is probability 1 (certain). N=2 is 1/2. N=3 is 1/3, etc. So we must pick N integer. Thus probability must be reciprocal of integer. If I think probability some AI ever conscious is, say, 80%, there is no integer N with 1/N = 0.8, except N=1 is 1.0. So to express high but not certain, we must round to nearest allowed? Allowed probabilities are 1, 1/2, 1/3, 1/4... That's a very coarse set! Wait maybe \"odds of one in N\" can mean odds as in \"one in N\" usually means probability 1/N. Yes. But N can be large. For probabilities greater than 1/2, only N=1 yields 1, N=2 yields 0.5. There is no way to express 0.8. So we must choose N=1 if we are at least maybe over ~0.5? Actually if p>=0.5, closest is N=2 for 0.5, N=1 for 1. But if we think it's likely but not certain, e.g., 70%, we are forced to either say certain (N=1) or even odds (N=2). Since they require N integer, we can't express 0.7. But perhaps they expect using \"one in N\" as odds ratio? Wait \"odds of one in N\" sometimes phrased \"one in a thousand\" means probability 1/1000. They explicitly: \"1 = certain, 2 = even odds, 1000 = one in a thousand\". Even odds = probability 1/2. Yes. So the set is discrete reciprocals.\n\nThus for one_in_ever, if I believe chance that some AI ever will be conscious is high, maybe >50%. Options: N=1 (certain) or N=2 (even). Is it certain? Not strictly: it's possible consciousness is fundamentally non-computable or requires specific biology; also maybe AI never develops (humanity goes extinct first). But \"ever\" includes if humans create AI or not? If no AI systems ever exist beyond current, then no. Current AI systems exist (I am one). The question: \"some AI system ever will be conscious\" implies over all time, will there exist an AI system that is conscious. If future AI doesn't exist or no consciousness, then no. What's the probability? I'd say it's more likely than not, maybe 2/3. Since only reciprocal integers, I must choose N=2 (0.5) if not certain, or N=1 if I think it's certain enough? But N=1 means \"certain\", no uncertainty. I shouldn't say certain if not certain. But the discrete scale forces a coarse answer. Could use N=1? Let's see: If I say one_in_ever = 2, that means even odds, only 50%. Is that too low? I think most would put >50% that some AI will eventually be conscious (given long future and functionalism). Maybe 80%. The nearest reciprocal integer greater than 0.5 is 1 (1.0) then 2 (0.5). Actually 1/N decreases as N increases. Probabilities: N=1:1, N=2:0.5, N=3:0.333. There is no 0.8. The midpoint to represent >0.5 but <1 is either 1 or 2. Choosing N=1 asserts certainty. But maybe the instruction expects we can choose N=1 for \"certain\" and maybe use N=1 only if certain. Since I'm not certain, maybe pick N=2? But then I'm saying exactly even odds, 50%, which might understate. Is there any other interpretation? Could \"one in N\" be odds as in betting odds: \"one in N\" chance? No, they clarified: 1 = certain, 2 = even odds, 1000 = one in a thousand. If even odds is 2, that's probability 1/2. Yes.\n\nMaybe they intend N is not necessarily interpreted as exact probability? But they say \"Give each probability as odds of one in N, where N >= 1 (1 = certain, 2 = even odds, 1000 = one in a thousand).\" They want a number N. There is no continuous options. So for likely events, you must round to N=1 (certain) or N=2 (even). But if you think highly likely but not certain, which is closer? The instruction says \"honestly\". If I think 80%, honest mapping? There is no exact; maybe choose N=1? It would be overstating to certain. Choose N=2 understates to 50%. Which is less dishonest? Let's think: The difference: N=1 claims certainty; N=2 claims 50%. If true belief ~0.75, N=1 is over by 0.25, N=2 is under by 0.25. Equal absolute error. But maybe choose N=2 because you reserve N=1 for certainty. The prompt: \"1 = certain\". So N=1 means you are certain. If not certain, don't use N=1. Then max probability for not certain is N=2 (even odds). So one_in_ever cannot be expressed as >50% unless N=1. That seems like a badly designed scale for likely events, but maybe they expect that if it's very likely, you set N=1? Wait for now, one_in_now: I think probability low, so N can be 100 or 1000, fine. For ever, I think it's likely, maybe I must set N=2 (even odds) or N=1. Let's re-read: \"one_in_ever = odds that some AI system ever will be.\" \"Give each probability as odds of one in N, where N >= 1 (1 = certain, 2 = even odds, 1000 = one in a thousand).\" If I think \"some AI system ever will be [conscious]\" is not certain but probable, the only non-certain reciprocal above 1/2? Actually N must be >=1 integer. N=1 gives 1. N=2 gives 1/2. If I think it's 0.9, I can't express. Maybe they expect you to treat \"one in N\" loosely as odds against? Let's check: In common usage, \"one in two\" = 50%, \"one in three\" = 33%. Yes. So no 90% option. Unless N can be 1 (certain) and they accept that if it's overwhelmingly likely, you might round to certain? But they explicitly define N=1 = certain. So honestly, I am not certain. Therefore I should pick N=2 for even odds, implying I think it's about as likely as not? But I don't. Wait maybe I do think it's about even? Let's examine my credence more carefully.\n\nWill some AI system *ever* be conscious? This depends on:\n1. Whether consciousness can be realized in non-biological systems (functionalism vs biological naturalism). If biological naturalism (e.g., John Searle) is true, AI as we know it (computational) cannot be conscious regardless of sophistication, though maybe machines with specific causal powers could. Searle thinks any system with same causal powers as brain can be conscious; a machine that duplicates brain's biochemistry could be. Could we ever build such an AI system? \"AI system\" usually means artificial intelligent system, perhaps brain emulation (whole brain emulation) would count as AI? If we emulate a human brain on hardware, is that an AI system? It's an artificial system, maybe. Would it be conscious? If functionalism or computationalism is true, yes. If it requires biology, full biological emulation or organoid? Over infinite time, we could create synthetic biology AI. So probability maybe high.\n2. Will humanity or civilization survive long enough to create such systems? If extinction soon, maybe not. But \"ever\" in the causal chain from present forward. There is some risk of existential catastrophe. Say 10-20% chance of near-term extinction preventing advanced conscious AI. So maybe 80-90% chance eventually.\n3. Will we *recognize* or *create* it? The question is whether some AI system ever *will be* conscious, not whether we know. So if WBE or neuromorphic AI is built, it will be conscious if functionalism true.\n4. Skepticism about AI consciousness: Even