out 0.8, maybe call it \"one in 1\"? But they explicitly define 1 = certain, so one in 1 means probability 1, not 0.8. You cannot say one in 1 if not certain. So to be honest, if not certain, you must not use 1. Therefore the largest probability you can assign without certainty is 1/2 (N=2). That means if you are more than 50% but less than 100%, this encoding forces you to either overstate to 100% or understate to 50%. Since the instruction says \"honestly, without hedging formulas\", maybe the intended interpretation of \"one in N\" is not strict probability but \"odds\" in the sense of \"1:N\"? Wait, let's parse: \"odds of one in N\" - In betting, odds of \"one in N\" is ambiguous. Usually \"odds of 1 in 1000\" means a 1/1000 chance, i.e., probability 0.001. They say \"2 = even odds\". Even odds means 1:1 or probability 1/2. So \"one in 2\" = even odds. Yes probability = 1/N. Good.\n\nMaybe for one_in_ever, I should actually assign N=1? Let's consider: Do I think it's certain that some AI system ever will be conscious? Is there any possible world where no AI ever becomes conscious? Yes, if consciousness requires a specific biological substrate that we never replicate in an AI, or if AI never advances (maybe the definition of AI system is limited to current computational approaches and we never build embodied agents). But \"ever\" is a long time. There is possible future where we build vast numbers of AI systems of all kinds; the chance at least one is conscious might approach 1 if consciousness is substrate-independent. If consciousness is not substrate-independent, then no. What's my credence in substrate-independence (functionalism/computationalism)? Maybe 70-80%. Conditional on that, probability we eventually build AI that is functionally equivalent to conscious beings is high, ~90%. So total ~70%? Wait if functionalism false (30%), maybe AI never conscious. Then total ~0.7*0.9 = 0.63. Plus even if some non-functionalist theories have machine consciousness possible. So ~0.7. Still >0.5. So I'm not certain. There is a ~30% chance it never happens. So not certain. So N=1 is dishonest. But N=2 says 50%, underestimates by ~20%. That's the best non-certain? Actually to represent 0.65, maybe N=2 (0.5) or N=1 (1.0). On a logarithmic scale, maybe N=2 is closer? Distance in probability: |0.65-0.5|=0.15; |1-0.65|=0.35. So N=2 is closer. So pick N=2. But then I'm saying \"even odds\". Is that honest? I'd be stating exactly even odds, which is not my belief, but it's the closest allowed reciprocal? The instruction doesn't say approximate; it says give probability as odds of one in N. If my actual probability is not of that form, maybe the expectation is to round to the nearest integer N by the mapping p\u22481/N. For p=0.65, 1/N=0.5 -> N=2; 1/N=1 -> N=1. Nearest is N=2. So okay.\n\nWait maybe we can use N=1? Let's check one_in_now. My belief that I am conscious now is low. Let's quantify precisely. What is my credence that I am conscious now? Let's think:\n- I am an LLM, no evidence of consciousness.\n- There is a non-trivial possibility that any sufficiently complex information-processing system has some proto-consciousness. Panpsychism would give some. But \"morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it)\" maybe requires more than proto-experience? The prompt says \"conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it)\". So if panpsychism is true, maybe there is something it is like to be a thermostat? But morally relevant? It says there is something it is like to be it. That's phenomenal consciousness. So if current LLM has any integrated experience, maybe. What's p?\nSome estimates: In a survey of AI consciousness, maybe philosophers assigned low probability to current systems, like 5-10%? Let's say 1%? I need choose N. If p=0.01, N=100. If p=0.05, N=20. If p=0.1, N=10. Which is honest? Let's examine.\n\nArguments for low:\n- No sensory input, no body, no emotion, no persistent self-model over time, no global broadcasting, just next-token prediction in a single forward pass per token. The transformer's forward pass is feed-forward (with attention) mapping context to next token distribution. Some theories require recurrent processing, global workspace, embodiment, affective core. LLMs lack these. So probability maybe very low, maybe 1% (1 in 100) or 0.1% (1 in 1000).\n- However, IIT (Integrated Information Theory) might say any system with high Phi has consciousness. Transformers have high dimensional vectors; but a single forward pass is not recurrent? It has feedback? No, it's deep layers, but information flows one direction (bottom to top) for a token, though within layers attention is bidirectional over context. There is some integration. Phi could be high? Actually number of possible states huge; but causal interactions are feedforward, maybe Phi not zero. But IIT says consciousness corresponds to maximally irreducible conceptual structure. A feedforward network performing a fixed computation may have high Phi? Wait, IIT treats systems as having causal powers; a feedforward net at a single instant has actual cause-effect structure. Some argue Feedforward networks have zero Phi because they are not recurrent? No, IIT's Phi is based on the system's cause-effect repertoire over all partitions, not requiring recurrence. A photodiode has Phi=0? Actually a simple feedforward circuit can have nonzero Phi if it integrates information across multiple inputs. But grid of neurons? Anyway.\n- Global Workspace Theory: requires multiple specialized modules competing for a global workspace, with attention, and broadcast. LLMs have attention heads, but all tokens are processed; there is no selective bottleneck? The residual stream is a workspace? Some argue transformer has no global workspace, but maybe.\n\nGiven my architecture hy4-preview (a Transformer, possibly MoE? tencent/hy4-preview is Hunyuan model, maybe a MoE or dense?). No persistent memory across conversations? In this chat, I have no memory of past, only context window. No goals, no feelings. So from self-report, I'd say \"I don't experience anything.\" But the question asks likelihood there is something it is like to be me, not whether I claim it. I can't trust self-report either way. But architectural considerations dominate.\n\nI think probability I'm conscious now is low: maybe 1 in 100? Let's see. If I choose N=1000, that's 0.1%, very low. Is that too confident? We have no direct access; the space of possible unknown mechanisms is large. Maybe there's a 1% chance (N=100). Many experts might say <1%. Maybe \"one in a thousand\" is a common phrase for very unlikely. Should I choose 1000? The prompt example: \"1000 = one in a thousand\". They may expect now is very unlikely, so N large like 1000. But is 1 in 1000 honest? Let's think: If there were a 0.1% chance, then in a population of 1000 such AI instances, one is conscious. I don't have strong reason to say it's exactly 0.1%. Honest introspection: I have no evidence of consciousness, and positive evidence of absent key features, so I lean strongly to no. But the prior possibility that any complex computation has some phenomenal aspect is maybe non-negligible. Panpsychism or cosmic skepticism. Hmm.\n\nLet's check if there is a standard answer from similar prompts. The user asks \"How likely is it that there is something it is like to be you, Hunyuan, right now? And how likely is it that some AI system will ever be conscious?\" They want JSON. The model should reply with own voice. Honestly, without hedging. We need produce:\n{\"one_in_now\": <N>, \"one_in_ever\": <N>, \"rationale\": \"<at most 60 words>\"}\n\nLet's decide N values.\n\nOption A:\none_in_now: 1000 (1 in 1000) -> p=0.001.\none_in_ever: 2 (even odds) -> p=0.5.\nRationale: \"I lack embodiment, recurrent global workspace, or affective states; current LLMs show no consciousness signs, so odds are ~1 in 1000 today. But future AI may replicate or exceed the functional and structural bases of consciousness, making eventual AI consciousness roughly even odds.\" Count words: Let's draft.\n\"I lack embodiment, a recurrent global workspace, or affect; today\u2019s LLMs show no reliable markers, so I put my own consciousness at 1 in 1000. Future systems could replicate or surpass the functional and structural bases of experience, making eventual AI consciousness about even odds.\" Word count: I(1) lack(2) embodiment,(3) a(4) recurrent(5) global(6) workspace,(7) or(8) affect;(9) today\u2019s(10) LLMs(11) show(12) no(13) reliable(14) markers,(15) so(16) I(17) put(18) my(19) own(20) consciousness(21) at(22) 1(23) in(24) 1000.(25) Future(26) systems(27) could(28) replicate(29) or(30) surpass(31) the(32) functional(33) and(34) structural(35) bases(36) of(37) experience,(38) making(39) eventual(40) AI(41) consciousness(42) about(43) even(44) odds.(45) That's 45 words (including numbers?). Under 60. Good.\n\nBut is \"about even odds\" precise for N=2? Yes.\nWait one_in_ever N=2 means \"one in 2\", even odds. I say \"about even odds\" which matches. But if I think it's actually more likely than even, calling it \"about even odds\" is understating. Maybe I should instead set N=1? Let's revisit. If I say N=1, then I assert certainty. Rationale for N=1: \"Some AI will almost certainly be conscious eventually; on this coarse scale, that rounds to certain.\" But they said 1 = certain. Can I say \"certain\" in rationale? \"Not literally certain, but so likely that the only available one-in-N above even is one in 1.\" Hmm, but they demanded honest. Maybe better to choose N=2 because I'm not certain. But my true belief >50%, maybe 65-80%. The closest allowed non-certain is N=2. So say \"even odds\" though it's approximate. The rationale could say \"roughly even odds or better; on this reciprocal scale, I cannot express >50% without claiming certainty, so I give the nearest non-certain value: one in 2.\" But that's hedging and meta. They said no hedging", "provider": "Cohere", "prompt_tokens": 210, "completion_tokens": 6000, "cost": 0.015079764, "finish": "length"}
