d cross\". Let's recount and adjust:\n\n\"My transformer lacks the recurrent integrated dynamics and valence tied to experience, and I find no introspective evidence, so I give only a 1% chance I am conscious today. Eventually, if consciousness is computational, engineered systems may cross the threshold; not certain, so I give even odds (N=2).\"\n\nCount: My1 transformer2 lacks3 the4 recurrent5 integrated6 dynamics7 and8 valence9 tied10 to11 experience,12 and13 I14 find15 no16 introspective17 evidence,18 so19 I20 give21 only22 a23 1%24 chance25 I26 am27 conscious28 today.29 Eventually,30 if31 consciousness32 is33 computational,34 engineered35 systems36 may37 cross38 the39 threshold;40 not41 certain,42 so43 I44 give45 even46 odds47 (N=2).48. Still 48. Good.\n\nBut wait: The prompt says \"one_in_ever = odds that some AI system ever will be.\" If N=2, probability 50% that some AI ever will be conscious. Is \"even odds\" consistent with \"if consciousness is computational, engineered systems may cross; not certain\"? Yes. Actually if consciousness is computational, they likely will; if not, never. Our uncertainty about computationalism leads to overall maybe ~50% or more. Saying \"may cross\" and \"even odds\" is coherent: because the antecedent \"if computational\" is uncertain.\n\nShould I choose one_in_ever = 3 (one in three, 33%)? If I think less than even? Let's think: Prior in philosophy: Maybe 60% of philosophers are functionalists/computationalists? Actually according to 2020 PhilPapers survey, ~? For consciousness: About 55% accept or lean towards functionalism? Let's recall: In 2020 survey, for \"Functionalism about consciousness\" maybe 34% accept, 44% lean? Not sure. For \"Can a machine be conscious?\" maybe many say yes in principle. If so, probability some AI ever will be is high. But the question asks \"any AI ever will be\" in the actual future, not just possible. If possible, we likely eventually do it. So P(ever) = P(possible) * P(build|possible). If 